A||||10||HF6582||Ball joints and poly bushinings||||5||
Z||000000||HF6582||12-18-1999||05:16 PM||||Hello all<P>Ordered new poly bushings for the front of my<BR>86GT.  Just wondering if anyone has any tips for installing or removal of the old bushings and ball joints.  I kinda figured that while I have the front end apart I would just replace everything with new parts.<P>Also what years was the WS6 package available and was the ride hiegth lower and what was the RPO code.<P>Thanks <BR>Scott<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||batboy||12-18-1999||10:30 PM||||I just did a complete front end rebuild on my Fiero last fall. You were mentioning front springs. This is the time to replace them if you've been planning that mod. I did the lower control arm poly bushings, ball joints, and springs first. Then after this was back together, stage two was to replace the sway bar bushings and end links with new poly ones. (This was easy except when I broke one of the bolts that goes up into the frame and had to drill it out and retap it.) You have to remove the sway bar end links to get to the lower control arm anyway. Stage three was to replace the upper control arm bushings and ball joints. Breaking it up into stages made it easier for me to tackle the job by myself.<P>Once the lower control arms were off, I took it to a chassis shop with a press and let them press out the old lower ball joints and press in new ones. I bought Prothane bushings which slip into the old metal bushing casing, so the easiest way to get the old bushing out is burn it out. I used a torch and alternated heat to the rubber of all four bushings, after a while it took off burning on it's own. Shortly after that I was able to push the burning bushings (sounds like something out of the Bible) out of the casings by using an old tire iron. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000002||HF6582||12-18-1999||11:57 PM||||Batboy,<BR>Thanks<BR>The reason I asked about the WS6 package is that my car sits lower than any other stock GT that I have seen.<P>I started take the control arms off today and the springs look like the have some sort of factory marking on them.  My car doesn't have a big gap between the tires and wheel wells like other cars.<P>Thanks <BR>Scott||||reg||5
Z||000003||batboy||12-19-1999||10:36 AM||||If the car sits just right, then excellent. Don't even bother doing anything with them. You don't have to remove the spring to do the job either.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||12-19-1999||10:54 AM||||As I recall...... the WS6 package will show that code on the code lable next to the brake master cyl. That would answer your question quick.<P>You could also go to pontiac historical service and order the document set for your car. It lists all the installed options amongst other things. They have a web site, but I don't have the address handy.||||reg||1
Z||000005||js||12-19-1999||09:49 PM||||I did the polyeurethane bushings on my '86 SE this past September. I had some problems removing the bolts for the bushings. The bolts were corroded in the steel liner of the original rubber bushing. Even though I was able to loosen the nuts, the bolts didn't come out. I was able to turn the bolts with a wrench freely, but all it was doing was turning the bolt with the steel linner inside the rubber bushing. Not much room to swing a hammer on the end of them either. I used alot of PBlaster penetrant, hammering, turning, and finally got them out. I did the rear bushings too. But I had my friend at work use our air impact wrenches on the bushing bolts and strut bolts to free them up before I did the removal.<P>Time to do? It took me a week, about 8 hrs. each day. I did the front upper and lower bushings and ball joints, rear bushings, shocks, struts, and front and rear sway bar bushings, and front and rear brakes. Also, I installed lowering springs.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GREENDANO||12-21-1999||12:35 PM||||Contol arm bushings on an open fire...   That's a nice Christmas song.<BR>For the record I just burned mine out yesterday in my project car.  Once they get lit they burn forever!  After they're out, hit the inside of the bushing sleeve in the control arm with the torch a little more to burn the rest of the rubber out.  That way there's no rust or rubber to clean out.  Oh yeah, they get real gooey after burning so pick 'em up with a screwdriver to throw them out afterwards.  <BR>Hey Batboy, did you get any to heat just right and launch across the driveway?  I was only able to get one of the eight on the front arms to shoot out.  I'll hit up the rears tonight or tomorrow.  I'll let you know.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>  BTW, buring the bushings is pretty much an outside job.  Ever see those tire fires on TV?  Rubber stinks and burns with an awful black smoke.  Unless you're really into blasting your lungs with crap, do this job outside.<BR>  Dan-O||||reg||10
Z||000007||batboy||12-21-1999||07:46 PM||||None of mine shot out. I nudged them out with an old tire iron after they were flaming good for a while. It was pretty easy, except I caught the sofa on fire trying to do it inside the house.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Monkeyman||12-21-1999||10:57 PM||||Pyromaniac...Er...I mean, batboy--didn't you think that maybe you should keep the fire outside?  Sorry, but I had to say something <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000009||GREENDANO||12-22-1999||07:08 PM||||That's why I did my second Fiero's control arms outside...I have new funiture that I didn't want to burn like the old stuff.  <BR>  BTW, just for safety sake, when I said outside I ment outside of the SHOP.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <BR>  Batboy, maybe next control arm you can pop a bushing out, It's pretty cool, funny too.  <BR>  Dan-O||||reg||1
Z||000010||RFMike||12-22-1999||10:42 PM||||Batboy,<BR>I just fell off my chair laughing!  <P>Watched any Red Green lately?||||reg||10
